<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sub-liouville-joseph" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Joseph Liouville (1809-1882)</title>
  <idx>Joseph Liouville</idx>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <alert>Citizenship:</alert> 🇫🇷 French
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Core Contributions:</alert>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <xref ref="def-action-classical-mechanics" text="custom">Classical mechanics definition for
            the action</xref>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-liouville-identity">
      <title>Liouville's Identity</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>F(x,y,z)</m> defined on integer triples <m>(x,y,z)\in\Z</m> such that:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>F</m> is <em>odd</em> in the variable <m>x</m>:
                <md>
                  F(-x,y,z)=-F(x,y,z) \, .
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>F</m> is <em>even</em> in the pair <m>(y,z)</m>:
                <md>
                  F(x,-y,-z)=F(x,y,z) \, .
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
          For every <m>n\in\N</m>, <term>Liouville's identity</term> states:
            <md>
              \sum_{a^2+bc=n}{\big[ 2F(c^2a,a+b,2a+2bc) \big]} = F(b + c, a, b-c) + 2T_1(n) + 2T_2(n)
            </md>
          where:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The summation is over all ordered triples <m>(a,b,c)</m> of integers satisfying <m>a^2+bc=n</m>,
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
               <p>
                 <m>T_1(n)=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}F(j,j,-j)</m> (with <m>j</m> is <em>odd</em>,
               </p>
              </li>
              <li>
               <p>
                 <m>T_2(n)=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}F(2,j,2j)</m> (with <m>j</m> is <em>even</em>,
               </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <remark>
        This identity leverages the parity properties of <m>F</m> to related sums over all possible solutions to sums
        over a subset of possible solutions.
      </remark>
      <remark>
        Used by <xref ref="sub-gibbs-josiah" text="custom">Josiah Gibbs</xref> in his derivation of Lioville's theorem.
      </remark>
    </theorem>

</subsection>

